THE BIG IDEA

A gold-nibbed fountain pen (https://thehustle.co/news/in-a-digital-world-pe=
ople-still-love-a-good-fountain-pen?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_whMP5hqTLehJmtV6jDpRt=
FP1ImfbW_N-v3JfAzVB2QQcoNes_fCgw3sZR3ylxgBgDH8F8Z2sOpe0ok8HlsWUr-KWpzw&_hsm=
i=3D395307565 )

In a digital world, people still love a good fountain pen

Did you get a fountain pen for Christmas? Was it from a fountain pen enthus=
iast trying to get you into the hobby? Because we know a guy=E2=80=A6

There=E2=80=99s something very satisfying about writing with a fountain pen=
, but they can be expensive. A Montblanc pen could set you back $1k+.

But there are several companies, largely in China, that produce knockoff ve=
rsions of pricier models, per (https://www.wsj.com/lifestyle/fountain-pens-=
chinese-replicas-montblanc-7f4d43d0?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_whMP5hqTLehJmtV6jDpRt=
FP1ImfbW_N-v3JfAzVB2QQcoNes_fCgw3sZR3ylxgBgDH8F8Z2sOpe0ok8HlsWUr-KWpzw&_hsm=
i=3D395307565 ) The Wall Street Journal, despite the ire of the pen makers =
they=E2=80=99re accused of copying.

- Jinhao offers pens starting at ~$12.

- Majohn Wancai pens start as low as $5 (https://thefabulousscientist.com/2=
024/06/09/fountain-pen-review-the-best-chinese-brand-fountain-pens-in-my-ex=
perience/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_whMP5hqTLehJmtV6jDpRtFP1ImfbW_N-v3JfAzVB2QQcoNe=
s_fCgw3sZR3ylxgBgDH8F8Z2sOpe0ok8HlsWUr-KWpzw&_hsmi=3D395307565 ) .

What=E2=80=99s so great about fountain pens?

They use a metal nib and refillable liquid ink, whereas the more common bal=
lpoint pen uses a rolling ball and an oil-based ink. Fountain pens, thus, r=
equire less pressure to use and allow for a smoother, easier writing experi=
ence that many find more enjoyable.

They=E2=80=99re also customizable, with many different sizes and kinds of n=
ibs, not to mention refillable inks. Some enthusiasts boast vast =E2=80=94 =
and expensive =E2=80=94 collections.

These enthusiasts=E2=80=A6

=E2=80=A6 have mixed feelings about the knockoffs. While some find them rep=
ugnant, others note that they=E2=80=99ve provided an entry point for new en=
thusiasts and collectors. A Reddit group for enthusiasts has doubled to ~36=
8k users over the past five years.

Artist and enthusiast Irv Tepper, who boasts a collection of 200+ pens, tol=
d WSJ he actually prefers the cheaper Chinese-made pens for everyday use an=
d to gift to people he hopes to introduce to his hobby.

Are there similar trends?

As our lives have become increasingly digitized, there=E2=80=99s been a ris=
ing interest in physical media (vinyl records, cassette tapes, board games,=
 etc.) and hobbies that involve using your hands or getting outdoors.

There=E2=80=99s also been a growing demand for stationery, a global market =
that=E2=80=99s grown since the pandemic, when people took up journaling and=
 calligraphy as hobbies, per (https://www.forbes.com/sites/katehardcastle/2=
025/03/02/the-rise-of-stationerycore-why-a-digital-generation-is-falling-in=
-love-with-analogue/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_whMP5hqTLehJmtV6jDpRtFP1ImfbW_N-v3Jf=
AzVB2QQcoNes_fCgw3sZR3ylxgBgDH8F8Z2sOpe0ok8HlsWUr-KWpzw&_hsmi=3D395307565 )=
 Forbes. Consumers shop for high-quality, beautifully designed cards, noteb=
ooks, planners, and other paper items, so it makes sense they=E2=80=99d wan=
t the pens to match.

And if a $12 pen feels just as lovely as a $200 splurge, well=E2=80=A6 who =
can blame them?

